  "body": "A follow-up from #3995.\n\n## Context\n\nThe fix for #3995 (branch `issue/3995-ui-link-stale-discovery`) makes each window hold an OS file lock on `<pid>.lock` while it runs. The CLI dials a link file only while that lock is held, before the hello and after it. This stops a false server on the freed port of a crashed window.\n\n## What remains\n\nThe lock proves that the window runs. It does not prove which socket on the TCP port answers. On Windows, a local process can bind the same loopback port with `SO_REUSEADDR` while the window runs and take a connection. A process that also reads the link file can then compute every token proof.\n\nOn Unix the link socket is in a 0700 folder. Only the same user can replace it, so this risk does not occur there.\n\n## Fix to decide\n\n- Replace the TCP port on Windows with a named pipe that has a user-only ACL.\n- Make the CLI ask the kernel which process serves the pipe (`GetNamedPipeServerProcessId`), and refuse each other peer before it sends the hello.\n- std has no named-pipe API. The fix needs a direct `windows-sys` dependency (it is in `Cargo.lock` already). This is a maintainer decision.\n- The design, its tests and two related bug fixes are on the branch of the closed PR #3971 (`issue/3874-link-proves-token`).\n\n## Done means\n\n- On Windows, a peer that is not the process named by the link file receives no hello and no tool call.\n- A test pins this case.\n",
